I remember that we've talked about this topic years ago, when I used DI in a console application I was working on. I submitted a talk on that for SymfonyLive, but you found it not interesting enough so we went for a different proposal of mine. And yet here we are. 😅

Next thing I remember is a discussion on Twitter where the author of another console application was very vocal on how they switched from Symfony DI to a different container because of performance issues that, as it turned out, could've been solved by not compiling the whole container on-the-fly whenever a CLI command was invoked. I don't want to reiterate this, but the learning I took from that incident is that you have to take a very steep learning curve only poorly supported by documentation when you want to use the component the right way without going through HttpKernel.

From what I recall (I don't have access to my application anymore on which I wanted to base my talk on), the DI component already got me very far out of the box. It was all there if you knew how to glue the pieces together.

The first problem was the whole bootstrapping process of the application. I first wrote some glue code that checked if the compiled container was already there and ran the compiler otherwise. But that approach wasn't very developer-friendly. I had to remember to always delete the container whenever I made changes to the application. All the nice cache invalidation logic we know from Symfony web applications was missing and hard to set up. I remember that I've borrowed some code from HttpKernel and FrameworkBundle to get closer to that level of DX.

I understand that this lack of DX and usability is the gap that you're attempting to close and that's great!

The second problem that I've had was that I lost access to Symfony's great ecosystem of bundles when developing applications without HttpKernel. You need logging in Symfony? Use MonologBundle! Oh, you need it in a console-only app? Okay, here's Monolog, here is a collection of glue code we call Monolog bridge, good luck assembling this! Bundles usually depend on HttpKernel, despite DI having its own extension system that we leverage and hide behind the bundle system.

The third problem was rather depolyment-related: Usually, you don't deploy a console application as you would deploy a web application. Instead, you probably would distribute the application including the compiled container as a PHAR file. And at that point I felt like I was completely on my own.

Not sure, how strongly we wan Symfony to become a framework for console applications, but I think we need to solved these three problems if we want that to happen.

